cy  don't linger on obtaining perfect  results at each round  thank you so much for taking the time to  listening to me today  looking forward to hearing your  questions and seeing how you would want  to use onyx in the future  thank you eileen for your informative  presentation we will now start the live  q a portion of the webinar  if you have a question you'd like to ask  please do so now just click on the ask a  question box located on the far left of  your screen we'll answer as many of your  questions as we have time for  all right let's get started  our first question here is a two-part  question and this asks how many fold  grader do i build the library compared  to my screening capacity is there a  point of diminishing returns  yeah thank you very much for this  question um to sort of answer this very  quickly the point of diminishing returns  starts when you are no longer  phenotyping unique isolates right  so what you want to understand is when  you're going to get to that point where  you're no longer going to be seeing  unique things to be able to do this  phenotyping  and so that helps answer the first part  of this question right so in general um  and and what we've talked through the  the entire seminar about is  how to recommend what sampling size that  you would use  and so in general we recommend sampling  your library at about a one to two fold  uh  screening depth larger than your library  size so this kind of means that  if you have a screen  say that allows you to sample ten  thousand isolates per day  then a library that's about five  thousand to ten thousand is a good match  for your screen  so this is something that really is is  at inscripto we've developed a lot of  phenotyping assay tools to best pair  these two things  but at the end of the day what's  important is that you get to the you  don't get to the point of those  diminishing returns  i hope that answers your question  great thank you looks like we have  another um sort of follo